(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cms-exam-1-master-exam-updated-form-b"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 1 Master Exam (Updated) — Form B","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 22-year-old man has painful erythema and warmth of the shoulders after a day outdoors, with no blistering and changes confined to the epidermis. How is this classified, and what course should be expected?","choices":["First degree; 1 to 2 weeks with infection risk","Second degree; prophylactic antibiotics","Second degree; 3 to 5 days","First degree; 3 to 5 days with desquamation","Third degree; surgical referral"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. First-degree sunburn produces erythema, warmth, and tenderness confined to the epidermis without blistering, and resolves in 3 to 5 days with desquamation. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy is seen for a recurrent, intensely pruritic rash. Examination shows poorly demarcated erythematous plaques with excoriations and lichenification of the antecubital and popliteal fossae. His mother reports a history of asthma. There is no fever, drainage, crusting, or tenderness. What is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Oral cephalexin","A topical corticosteroid plus regular emollient","Emollients alone","Oral terbinafine","Systemic prednisone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Treatment combines barrier repair with inflammation control: low to medium potency topical corticosteroid for the body, applied sparingly, with an emollient applied after rinsing. Neither element alone is adequate, which is what makes this the complete answer. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with a lifetime of outdoor work has multiple rough, scaly erythematous papules on the scalp and dorsal hands, each 2 to 8 mm, that feel like sandpaper when palpated. What molecular event is the critical step in their development?","choices":["Hyperinsulinaemia driving keratinocyte growth","Ultraviolet-induced melanocyte proliferation","Immunoglobulin A complex deposition","Loss of elastic tissue","Ultraviolet-induced TP53 mutation"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Actinic keratoses are intraepidermal keratinocytic dysplasias induced by cumulative ultraviolet B exposure, and ultraviolet-induced mutations in TP53 are the critical molecular event. They represent a field cancerisation process. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with plaque psoriasis of the elbows and knees is prescribed calcipotriene. What is the most important clinical consideration for this agent?","choices":["A vitamin D analogue; caution over nephrotoxicity","A retinoid; irritation and photosensitivity","A keratolytic used second line","A vitamin D analogue; risk of atrophy","A calcineurin inhibitor for the face"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Calcipotriene is a vitamin D analogue described as having the quickest action among the first-line options, with caution regarding nephrotoxicity. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 163"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a translucent skin-coloured papule over the distal interphalangeal joint of her index finger, with a longitudinal groove in the adjacent nail. What is the nature of this lesion?","choices":["An overgrowth of dense fibrous tissue","A pseudo-cyst of mucin extruded from the joint","A collection of lipid-laden macrophages","A true cyst lined by epithelium","An eccrine duct neoplasm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Digital mucous cysts are pseudo-cysts without a cellular lining or true capsule. They represent extrusion of mucinous contents from a local joint space into the surrounding dermis, typically over the distal interphalangeal joint, and may distort the nail. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has a sharply circumscribed, darkly pigmented papule under 7 mm on the thigh. What is this lesion also called, and what is its typical demographic?","choices":["Blue nevus, adolescence, dorsal hands","Spitz nevus, children, face and neck","Congenital melanocytic nevus, from birth","Reed nevus, lower extremities in the thirties","Dysplastic naevus, irregular borders"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pigmented spindle cell nevus, also called Reed nevus, occurs commonly in the thirties with females affected more than males, found on the extremities and mainly the lower extremities, especially the thigh. It is a sharply circumscribed darkly pigmented papule usually less than 7 mm. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has fine red lines on both cheeks that disappear when pressed with a glass slide. How is a telangiectasia defined, and what is its behaviour on pressure?","choices":["A congenital malformation","A dilated capillary under 1 mm that does not blanch","A capillary proliferation forming a papule","A 1 to 2 mm blood deposit that does not blanch","A dilated capillary under 1 mm that blanches"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Telangiectasias are acquired vascular lesions consisting of permanently dilated capillaries under 1 mm. They blanch, may be single or grouped with a central punctum, may be primary or secondary, and are associated with numerous diseases. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"A 45-year-old fair-skinned woman reports facial flushing that worsens with sun exposure, hot drinks, and alcohol. Examination shows persistent central facial erythema with telangiectasias across the cheeks and nose, and she describes her skin as stinging easily. There are no papules, no pustules, and no comedones. What subtype does she have?","choices":["Erythematotelangiectatic rosacea","Phymatous rosacea","Papulopustular rosacea","Ocular rosacea","Acne vulgaris"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The erythematotelangiectatic subtype produces persistent central facial erythema, flushing, and telangiectasias with sensitive stinging skin, and specifically no papules or pustules. It is the most common subtype. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A newborn boy is born with a large pigmented plaque over the posterior scalp and upper back. What relationship governs melanoma risk, and what additional evaluation should be considered?","choices":["Smaller lesions carry more risk; no imaging","Risk is unrelated to size; no imaging","It regresses spontaneously; observe","Larger lesions carry more risk; always excise fully","Larger lesions carry more risk; image brain and spine"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Congenital melanocytic naevi arise from somatic mutations and are evident at birth or shortly after. The larger the lesion, the higher the risk for melanoma. With naevi on the head or axial locations, neurocutaneous melanosis must be considered, and magnetic resonance imaging of the brain with or without the total spine is obtained concordant with the anatomic location. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman with ulcerative colitis develops a painful pustule on the shin that has enlarged over five days into a 6 cm ulcer with an undermined violaceous border. Wound cultures are negative. A surgical team proposes sharp debridement. What is the most important management principle?","choices":["A dry adherent dressing twice daily","Ankle-brachial index, then compression","Debride to remove devitalised tissue","Intravenous antibiotics as definitive therapy","Avoid debridement"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Debridement must be avoided in pyoderma gangrenosum because pathergy — worsening with trauma — is a defining feature. Wound care uses moist dressings and non-adherent contact layers instead. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with limited health literacy is being offered several treatment options with different risks. Why must clinicians be able to communicate evidence on prognosis, treatment, testing, and prevention effectively?","choices":["To help patients understand their risks and options","To shorten the consultation","To ensure patients accept the chosen plan","To transfer the decision to the patient","To satisfy documentation requirements"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Healthcare providers must be able to effectively communicate evidence on prognosis, treatments, diagnostic testing, and prevention to help patients understand their risks and options. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 20-year-old man who keeps a pet tarantula presents with itching of both forearms and a red, irritated eye. What cutaneous and ocular effects can tarantulas cause?","choices":["Embedded hairs; conjunctivitis or corneal granuloma","No cutaneous or ocular effects","Muscle spasm and abdominal pain","Necrotic ulceration from cytotoxic venom","A serpentine advancing track"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tarantula hairs may land and embed on skin or eyes when the animal is threatened, producing cutaneous responses ranging from mild local pruritus to granulomatous reactions, and conjunctivitis or corneal granuloma in the eye. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man arrives hypotensive and confused with an atypical presentation, and the team begins treating his signs and symptoms before a diagnosis is established. In which situation is the naturalistic, event-driven approach most appropriate?","choices":["A screening programme","A research setting appraising evidence","An unstable patient with an atypical presentation","Any situation at all","A stable outpatient with a classic presentation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The naturalistic or event-driven approach treats signs and symptoms before a definitive diagnosis. It is used mostly in emergency medicine, for unstable patients and atypical presentations, to rule out the worst-case scenario, following responses to interventions. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 5-year-old boy has multiple small flesh-coloured papules scattered over the trunk, and the clinician examines each one closely. What is the characteristic morphological feature of a molluscum contagiosum lesion?","choices":["Central umbilication of a pearly dome-shaped papule","A rough grey surface with thrombosed capillaries","An annular plaque with central clearing","A stuck-on look with comedone-like openings","Grouped vesicles on an erythematous base"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lesions are discrete, smooth-surfaced, firm, flesh-coloured, dome-shaped pearly papules averaging 3 to 5 mm, and central umbilication is characteristic. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 134"},{"q":"A 3-year-old boy with atopic dermatitis has a father with asthma and a mother with allergic rhinitis. What is the underlying epidermal abnormality most closely associated with his condition?","choices":["Autoantibodies against keratinocyte adhesion","Malassezia overgrowth in sebaceous areas","T cells infiltrating the hair follicle","Reduced filaggrin impairing the barrier","Autoantibody damage to the basement membrane"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Atopic dermatitis involves epidermal barrier impairment and immune dysregulation, most notably reduced filaggrin production, on a background of genetic susceptibility and an altered cutaneous microbiome. The atopic triad is atopic dermatitis, asthma, and allergic rhinitis. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man has greasy yellow scale over the scalp, eyebrows, and nasolabial folds. What organism is responsible for seborrheic dermatitis, and where is the condition typically found?","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus colonisation","Malassezia overgrowth at flexural sites","Malassezia overgrowth at sebum-rich sites","An acquired dermatophyte infection","Autoimmunity against sebaceous glands"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Seborrheic dermatitis is caused by overgrowth of Malassezia, which is normal skin flora. It is typically found on the scalp as dandruff, and on the eyebrows, under facial hair, in the nasolabial creases, forehead, nose, behind the ears, and in the external auditory canal. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"A clinician evaluating a 58-year-old man for a condition that would be dangerous to miss wants a test that will correctly identify patients who do have the condition, minimising the number of affected patients missed. What property is she describing?","choices":["Specificity","Pretest probability","Positive predictive value","Posttest probability","Sensitivity"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Sensitivity is the probability that the test shows a person has the condition when they do have it — how well a test detects a condition. Sensitivity is related to fewer false negatives. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with diabetes has an asymptomatic brownish patch in the crural region and scaling between the fourth and fifth toes. Under a Wood's lamp the affected skin fluoresces coral-red. What is the causative organism, and what is first-line treatment for localised disease?","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus; topical mupirocin","Candida albicans; topical miconazole","Corynebacterium minutissimum; topical erythromycin","Corynebacterium minutissimum; oral erythromycin","A dermatophyte; topical terbinafine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Erythrasma is a chronic superficial infection of intertriginous skin caused by Corynebacterium minutissimum invading the upper third of the stratum corneum. Coral-red fluorescence under a Wood's lamp is diagnostic, and first-line treatment for localised disease is topical erythromycin or clindamycin. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman with biopsy-confirmed erythema nodosum has bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy on chest radiograph together with fever and arthralgia. What is the most appropriate referral?","choices":["Pulmonology or rheumatology, for probable Löfgren syndrome","Gastroenterology, for probable inflammatory bowel disease","Infectious disease, for probable endemic mycosis","Dermatology, for repeat biopsy","Haematology, for probable lymphoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Löfgren syndrome is the association referred to pulmonology or rheumatology. The combination of erythema nodosum, bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, fever, and arthralgia is its recognised presentation. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has an expanding annular eruption whose appearance has become atypical and which flares every time a topical steroid is stopped. What has occurred, and what is the first management step?","choices":["Tinea incognito; stop the corticosteroid","Contact allergy; patch test first","Psoriasis unmasked; start calcipotriene","Tinea incognito; increase the steroid potency","Steroid atrophy; emollient and observe"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tinea incognito is tinea with a clinically altered appearance due to inappropriate treatment, usually topical steroids. Management begins by stopping the inappropriate corticosteroid or calcineurin inhibitor, then obtaining potassium hydroxide microscopy or culture from an active edge. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman has a basal cell carcinoma on the nasal ala that has recurred after a previous excision. When should Mohs micrographic surgery be considered for basal cell carcinoma?","choices":["High-risk sites, recurrence, or aggressive histology","Only for tumours over 5 cm","Every basal cell carcinoma","Only after topical therapy fails twice","Never; surgery is not required"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mohs surgery is indicated for high-risk anatomic sites, recurrent tumours, aggressive histology, or where tissue-sparing is needed. The overall goal is complete eradication with minimal cosmetic and functional deformity. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 17-year-old girl reports excessive sweating of both palms, soles, and axillae since early adolescence. She reports no sweating during sleep. Her father had the same problem. Thyroid studies and glucose are normal. What does this pattern indicate, and what is the appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Secondary; endocrine investigation","Primary focal; topical aluminium chloride","Anxiety disorder; psychological therapy","Frey syndrome; topical glycopyrronium","Primary focal; botulinum toxin A"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Absence of nocturnal sweating and symmetry favour primary hyperhidrosis, which begins in adolescence, is bilateral and focal, and often follows an autosomal dominant family pattern. Aluminium chloride 20 percent applied nightly to dry skin is first-line topical therapy. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 9-year-old boy has head lice that persisted after a first pediculicide application. Why is a multimodal approach recommended, and what does the World Health Organization recommend regarding pediculicide assessment?","choices":["Head lice are self-limiting","Essential oils are first line","Oral antibiotics are always needed","Resistance is rising; read the result at 24 hours","Resistance is falling; read it immediately"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A multimodal approach is warranted because of increasing resistance, and the World Health Organization recommends that pediculicidal testing be read 24 hours after application. If treatment is not followed appropriately, patients tend to seek alternative care such as essential oils. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has red, itchy, circular patches on the forearm, and the clinician is distinguishing tinea corporis from nummular eczema. What is the distinguishing feature?","choices":["Only nummular eczema affects the limbs","Nummular eczema clears centrally; tinea does not","Only tinea corporis is well demarcated","Only tinea corporis is pruritic","Tinea corporis clears centrally; eczema does not"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Both present as red or pink itchy circular patches. Tinea corporis is a fungal infection with an annular appearance, whereas nummular eczema is non-infectious and coin-shaped without central clearing. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with obesity has multiple soft, skin-coloured pedunculated papules on the neck and in the axillae, ranging from 2 to 6 mm. She asks what they are. What education is appropriate?","choices":["A marker of internal malignancy","They resolve on their own","Harmless growths where skin rubs together","Premalignant lesions requiring excision","Viral warts spread by contact"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Skin tags are fibroepithelial pedunculated papillomas that are harmless growths of normal skin, forming where skin rubs together such as the armpit, neck, under the breasts, and groin. They are very common, present in a large majority by age 70, and more frequent in women and in obesity. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man who has lost 9 kg without trying is found to have new velvety hyperpigmented thickening of the axillae, neck, and, unusually, the palms and oral mucosa, which developed rapidly over three months. He is not obese and his fasting glucose is normal. What is the most appropriate concern?","choices":["Paraneoplastic acanthosis nigricans","Insulin-resistant acanthosis nigricans","A niacin or corticosteroid effect","Postinflammatory hyperpigmentation","Confluent and reticulated papillomatosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Malignant acanthosis nigricans is paraneoplastic, especially with gastrointestinal adenocarcinoma. Rapid onset, unusual sites including the palms and mucosa, and the absence of obesity or insulin resistance in an older patient with weight loss all point away from the common benign form. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man presents with vesicles in the right ophthalmic division distribution, including lesions on the tip of his nose. What does that finding signify, and what should be done?","choices":["Hutchinson sign; start antivirals immediately","Ramsay Hunt syndrome","Zoster sine herpete","Disseminated zoster","Hutchinson sign; its absence excludes eye disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Herpes zoster ophthalmicus involves the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve. Hutchinson sign — lesions on the tip or side of the nose — increases ocular risk, but its absence does not exclude eye involvement, and systemic antiviral therapy should be started immediately. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man with acquired immunodeficiency syndrome has red-purple plaques on the trunk and on the hard palate. What causes Kaposi sarcoma, and what does it arise from?","choices":["Human herpesvirus 8, in blood and lymph vessel lining","Human immunodeficiency virus, in endothelium","Ultraviolet damage to dermal fibroblasts","Malignant T cells migrating to skin","Human papillomavirus, in keratinocytes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Kaposi sarcoma develops in the cells lining blood and lymph vessels and is associated with human herpesvirus 8. It produces red or purple macules, plaques, or nodules on skin or mucous membranes and can involve internal organs. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 4-month-old has a large facial hemangioma that is beginning to obstruct the visual axis of one eye. What is first-line treatment?","choices":["Immediate surgical excision in all cases","Systemic corticosteroids as first-line therapy","Serial observation alone","An oral beta-blocker such as propranolol","Pulsed dye laser as the primary treatment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Beta-blockers are first-line treatment, for example oral propranolol or timolol. Indications for treatment include cosmetic concern, functional involvement such as blocked vision, deep ulceration, and infection. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with moderate hidradenitis suppurativa of both axillae has not responded to topical therapy. What is the rationale for adding spironolactone?","choices":["An antibiotic suppressing the organism","It shrinks sinuses when injected into the lesion","A retinoid normalising keratinisation","An immunosuppressant blocking tumour necrosis factor","An aldosterone antagonist that lowers androgens"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Spironolactone is an aldosterone antagonist that inhibits ovarian and adrenal production of androgens. Combination birth control pills are used similarly, reducing luteinising hormone and follicle-stimulating hormone secretion. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"A 50-year-old labourer has a thickened, dry, scaly right palm that he has attributed to hard physical work. He also has scaling on both soles. What is the likely diagnosis, and how does it typically present?","choices":["Palmar psoriasis","Chronic paronychia","Tinea manuum, often one palm and both soles","Irritant contact dermatitis","Dyshidrotic eczema"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tinea manuum is associated with tinea pedis, and both palms and soles can be infected at the same time. Patients are often unaware of the infection, believing the changes are due to dry skin or hard physical labour, and the involved palm is thickened, dry, and scaly. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man presents with several intensely itchy patches on his lower legs that appeared over two months. Examination shows multiple well-demarcated, coin-shaped erythematous plaques 1 to 5 cm in diameter scattered over both thighs and lower legs, with prominent scale and crusting. Each plaque is uniform in appearance throughout. What single examination finding most strongly separates this from the principal alternative diagnosis?","choices":["The plaques are intensely pruritic rather than painless","The plaques are uniform throughout, without central clearing","The plaques are well demarcated rather than poorly demarcated","The plaques are located on the extremities rather than the trunk","The plaques are round rather than irregular in outline"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The main differential is tinea corporis, and the slide draws the line exactly here: tinea corporis has central clearing and nummular eczema does not. Uniform appearance across the whole plaque is what makes this nummular eczema. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"A furuncle on the neck of a 37-year-old man is incised and drained, and the culture grows methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. Which oral regimens are appropriate?","choices":["Ciprofloxacin or acetic acid soaks","Penicillin V or amoxicillin","Dicloxacillin or cephalexin","Topical mupirocin alone","Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, doxycycline, or clindamycin"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. When methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is cultured or suspected, the oral options given are trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, doxycycline, and clindamycin. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman develops an itchy pustular eruption two days after using a hotel hot tub. Examination shows follicular papules and pustules over the trunk, buttocks, and proximal limbs, sparing the face, neck, palms, and soles. She is afebrile. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Oral trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole","Topical mupirocin three times daily","Shower after hot tub use to prevent it","Reassurance; most resolve in 2 to 10 days","Oral ciprofloxacin for all patients"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pseudomonas folliculitis is usually self-limiting and most cases resolve without specific treatment, clearing in 2 to 10 days. A wet dressing of 5 percent acetic acid may be applied for 20 minutes 2 to 4 times daily for symptom relief. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman presents three days after a manicure with rapid onset of redness, swelling, and tenderness of the tissue around her right index fingernail. Purulent fluid has collected under the skin of the nail fold. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Oral acyclovir for herpetic whitlow","A topical antifungal and dry hands","Warm soaks three times daily alone","Nail plate removal","Incision and drainage with oral antibiotics"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Acute paronychia starts as cellulitis and progresses to abscess. Severe cases with a purulent collection require incision and drainage, cultures where appropriate to rule out methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus, and oral antibiotics such as amoxicillin-clavulanate or cephalexin. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man has mild plaque psoriasis affecting the elbows and knees. He has not used any treatment. What is the most appropriate first-line approach?","choices":["A biologic agent","Emollients, topical steroids, and calcipotriene","Salicylic acid and coal tar","A tapering course of oral prednisone","Methotrexate immediately"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. First-line treatment of mild plaque psoriasis is emollients, topical steroids, and calcipotriene, a vitamin D analogue with the quickest action, with phototherapy using ultraviolet B best for smaller stubborn areas. Salicylic acid and coal tar are second line. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 163"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with a lifetime of outdoor work has multiple 0.3 to 0.5 cm pink papules on the scalp and dorsal hands. Several are more apparent to touch than to sight. One lesion is thick, indurated, and has begun to bleed. What does that single lesion most require?","choices":["Liquid nitrogen cryotherapy","Reassurance","Observation for one year","Field-directed therapy","Biopsy"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The most important distinction for actinic keratosis is early squamous cell carcinoma or carcinoma in situ, especially when a lesion is thick, indurated, ulcerated, enlarging, painful, bleeding, persistent, or recurrent. Those features change the lesion from one to treat destructively to one to sample. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 16-year-old girl starting treatment for acne of the face and back asks how quickly it will work. What education is accurate?","choices":["Response cannot be measured objectively","Improvement within one week","4 to 6 weeks; back and chest take 3 to 4 months","No improvement for at least six months","4 to 6 weeks, with all sites responding equally"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Improvement in lesions can take 4 to 6 weeks, clinical improvement is measured by the number of new lesions after 6 to 8 weeks of therapy, and areas on the back and chest are slowest to respond, taking 3 to 4 months. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman with numerous naevi asks how she should check her own moles between clinic visits. What do the letters of the ABCDE recognition rule stand for?","choices":["Age, burn history, complexion, dysplasia, exposure","Asymmetry, bleeding, crusting, depth, erosion","Asymmetry, border, colour, diameter, evolution","Abscess, bulla, cyst, dermatitis, erythema","Asymmetry, border, colour, dermoscopy, excision"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Asymmetry means one half does not match the other; border refers to irregular, notched, or poorly defined edges; colour describes variegation with brown, red, white, black, or blue within one lesion; diameter and evolution complete the rule. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman has had urticarial lesions for two months. Individual lesions persist for more than 24 hours and resolve leaving brownish discoloration. She reports arthralgia. What should be suspected, and what test supports it?","choices":["Mastocytosis; positive Darier sign","Anaphylaxis; raised tryptase","Urticarial vasculitis; low complement","Hereditary angioedema; low C4","Chronic urticaria; normal complement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Urticarial vasculitis is distinguished by lesions persisting beyond 24 hours that leave bruising, with low complement levels and a biopsy showing leukocytoclastic vasculitis. Ordinary wheals blanch fully and last under 24 hours. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man has a dermatomal vesicular eruption and a student asks where zoster most often appears. Which dermatomes are most frequently involved?","choices":["No dermatome predominates","Cranial about 55 percent, thoracic about 20 percent","Sacral in the majority","Lumbar in the majority","Thoracic about 55 percent, cranial about 20 percent"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Thoracic dermatomes are most frequently involved at about 55 percent, cranial at about 20 percent, with lumbar and sacral distributions following. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"An 18-year-old boy is prescribed a topical antibiotic for acne, and benzoyl peroxide is added to the regimen. Why is benzoyl peroxide recommended alongside topical or oral antibiotic therapy for acne?","choices":["It provides ultraviolet protection","It inhibits tyrosinase and lightens pigment","It decreases sebum excretion","It is comedolytic and normalises keratinisation","It suppresses Cutibacterium and limits resistance"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Benzoyl peroxide is keratolytic and anti-inflammatory and suppresses Cutibacterium acnes. It is recommended alongside antimicrobials specifically to reduce the risk of bacterial resistance, and combination products exist for that purpose. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 12-year-old has several elevated, round papules under 1 cm with a rough greyish surface on the fingers. Trimming one reveals tiny red-black dots. What do those dots represent, and what does their presence help exclude?","choices":["Melanocytic nests, excluding melanoma","Deep roots, confirming the need for excision","Fungal elements, excluding tinea manuum","Keratin plugs, distinguishing it from a keratosis","Thrombosed capillaries, distinguishing it from a callus"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Tiny red or black dots represent thrombosed dilated capillaries, and trimming the surface makes them more prominent. A callus lacks them, which is why paring the lesion is a useful bedside manoeuvre. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 142"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man presents with a new benign lesion on a sun-exposed area of the forearm. What opportunity does this create?","choices":["Counselling on sunscreen and self-examination","Referral for full-body photography","Advising complete avoidance of outdoors","Reassurance alone","Prophylactic excision of all lesions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. When discussing any new lesion in sun-exposed areas, the clinician should take the opportunity to counsel on sunscreen use, avoiding direct sun during peak hours, and performing periodic skin self-examinations. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man presents with recurrent painful red nodules and draining lesions in both axillae and the groin. He has had four such episodes in the past six months and two lesions previously drained. He is obese and smokes. Examination shows inflamed nodules and several sinus tracts. What three elements establish the diagnosis?","choices":["Typical lesions, distribution, positive culture","Fever, leukocytosis, and a draining sinus","One abscess with a family history","Typical lesions, axilla and groin, recurrence","Typical lesions, distribution, and biopsy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hidradenitis suppurativa is primarily a clinical diagnosis requiring three key elements: typical lesions, characteristic distribution in the axilla and groin, and recurrence more than twice in six months. Biopsy is not usually required. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A Wood's lamp examination is performed on a 6-year-old girl with suspected tinea capitis and shows no fluorescence. What is the correct interpretation?","choices":["It indicates a bacterial cause","It excludes tinea capitis","It confirms Microsporum infection","It means oral therapy can be avoided","It excludes nothing; Trichophyton rarely fluoresces"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. A Wood lamp may rapidly support Microsporum infection, but Trichophyton tonsurans — the most common species — usually does not fluoresce. Confirmation rests on potassium hydroxide microscopy and fungal culture. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with toxic epidermal necrolysis is admitted to the burn unit. What scoring system should be calculated within 24 hours and repeated on day three?","choices":["The Fitzpatrick scale","The ankle-brachial index","The Parkland formula","The minimal erythema dose","SCORTEN"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. SCORTEN should be calculated within 24 hours of admission and repeated on day 3. Each variable scores one point, including age over 40 years, presence of malignancy, heart rate over 120 beats per minute, and the initial extent of epidermal detachment. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man presents with thick scaly plaques on both elbows and knees that have been present for years. Examination shows well-demarcated salmon-pink plaques with adherent silvery scale over the extensor elbows and knees, the sacrum, and the scalp. A linear plaque has developed along a surgical scar on his forearm. When scale is lifted from one plaque, pinpoint bleeding appears. What is this bleeding phenomenon called?","choices":["Auspitz sign","Darier sign","Koebner phenomenon","Nikolsky sign","Wickham striae"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Auspitz sign is the appearance of bleeding when scale is removed from a psoriatic plaque, produced by the dilated capillaries lying immediately beneath a thinned suprapapillary epidermis. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 159"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman has a firm 8 mm nodule on her lower leg with a brown halo and a slightly raised centre. When the skin on either side is squeezed together, the lesion dimples downward. What is this sign, and what does dermoscopy typically show?","choices":["Auspitz sign","Nikolsky sign","The dimple sign; peripheral pigment network","Darier sign","The dimple sign, seen in lipoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The dimple sign is characteristic of dermatofibroma, which is a firm 0.5 to 1 cm nodule formed by dense clusters of dermal fibroblasts, most commonly on the legs. Dermoscopy often shows a peripheral pigment network with a central white area. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman has widespread tense bullae and a pruritic prodrome. The clinician plans a biopsy. What specimen and technique will best establish the diagnosis?","choices":["Potassium hydroxide of blister fluid","A shave biopsy of the blister roof","Patch testing for an allergen","Lesion for histology, perilesional for immunofluorescence","Bulla centre for immunofluorescence alone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bullous pemphigoid is diagnosed by biopsying the lesion for histopathology and perilesional tissue for direct immunofluorescence examination. Serum indirect immunofluorescence or enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay can identify anti-basement membrane zone antibodies. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man reports six weeks of severe itching that is worst at night. Examination shows excoriations and eczematous change of the finger webs, the sides of the fingers, the volar wrists, the axillae, and the scrotum. The head and neck are spared. What is the pathognomonic lesion of this condition?","choices":["A thin, thread-like linear or J-shaped burrow 1 to 10 mm in length","Thick flaking scale containing millions of mites","Linear excoriations on the back, neck, shoulders, and waist","Excoriated eczematous dermatitis of the finger webs","Intense nocturnal pruritus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The pathognomonic lesion of scabies is a thin, thread-like linear or J-shaped burrow 1 to 10 mm long, a tunnel caused by movement of the mite in the stratum corneum. It is best seen in the interdigital webs and wrists, and is hard to see once excoriated. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 5-year-old girl is diagnosed with atopic dermatitis and a student asks how common the condition is and who it affects. What statement is most accurate?","choices":["More common in females than males","About 2 percent of children","It resolves in all by adolescence","About 20 percent of children; often begins early","About 20 percent; never begins after childhood"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Atopic dermatitis affects 20 percent of children worldwide and often begins in infancy or childhood. It is more common in males than females, may improve, persist, or recur, and adult-onset disease also occurs though it is rare. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman presents with an itchy rash on her forearm. She had a laceration dressed with an adhesive bandage four days ago. Examination shows a sharply demarcated rectangular erythematous plaque with tiny vesicles corresponding exactly to the adhesive portions of the dressing, and a rectangle of normal skin in the centre where the cotton pad had lain. The laceration itself is clean and healing. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Herpes simplex virus reactivation","Cellulitis of the forearm","Irritant contact dermatitis from the wound cleanser","Wound infection of the underlying laceration","Allergic contact dermatitis to the adhesive"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Patients allergic to the adhesive in an adhesive bandage present with a bandage-shaped rash that spares the middle area where the cotton lay. The eruption reproduces the shape of the contactant, which is the signature of allergic contact dermatitis. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 3-day-old newborn has pink irregular macules on the nape of the neck and the glabella that become more noticeable when he cries. What is this lesion?","choices":["A port-wine stain","Nevus simplex","A cherry angioma","An infantile hemangioma","A pyogenic granuloma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nevus simplex is a congenital vascular lesion, a variant of nevus flammeus involving more superficial dermal capillaries. It is most common on the head and neck, presents as pink to erythematous irregular macules or patches, and becomes more noticeable when the infant cries. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a melanoma on the calf with a Breslow thickness of 1.4 mm and is being planned for definitive local treatment. What are the recommended re-excision margins for melanoma by thickness?","choices":["1 cm in situ, 2 cm under 1 mm, 3 cm over 1 mm","5 mm for every melanoma","Margins set by lesion diameter","No re-excision after the biopsy","0.5 to 1 cm in situ, 1 cm under 1 mm, 1 to 2 cm over"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Definitive local treatment uses a 0.5 to 1 cm margin for in situ disease, 1 cm for tumours under 1 mm, and 1 to 2 cm for tumours over 1 mm. Expert-centre referral applies to melanoma deeper than 1 mm or with lymph node involvement. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 44-year-old runner has a painful lesion on the plantar surface of the forefoot. Pressing straight down on it reproduces the pain, whereas squeezing it from the sides does not. The skin lines run through the lesion, and paring it reveals a central cone of hard keratin. What is the diagnosis?","choices":["Callus","Clavus, a corn with a central hyperkeratotic core","Epidermoid cyst","Verruca vulgaris","Plantar fibroma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A clavus results from pressure on a localised area, creating a cone-shaped central core of hard keratin. Pain occurs on direct downward pressure, and the skin lines run through the lesion. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man treated for scabies returns with new pustules and continuing pruritus. What complications of scabies should be anticipated?","choices":["Permanent scarring alopecia","Systemic parasitic infection of internal organs","Malignant transformation of the burrow","Staphylococcal superinfection and post-scabietic papules","Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis in all"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcal superinfection may lead to sepsis and requires antibiotics. Persistent pruritic post-scabietic papules are treated with mid to high potency corticosteroids or intralesional triamcinolone acetonide. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 31-year-old camper develops a prickling sensation lasting about 30 minutes after wading in a freshwater lake, followed 10 to 12 hours later by severe itching and then erythematous papules. What is this condition, and what completes its life cycle?","choices":["Cutaneous larva migrans; from contaminated soil","Cercarial dermatitis; snails complete the cycle","Tungiasis; a flea penetrating the foot","Scabies; a burrowing mite","Bedbug bites from campsite bedding"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cercarial dermatitis, also called swimmer's itch or clam digger's itch, results from penetration of the skin by cercarial forms of parasitic flatworms. Host animals such as waterfowl and muskrats pass eggs in feces into water, the eggs hatch and infect snails within 12 hours, and cercariae are released about 5 weeks later. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 38-year-old farmer has tender, boggy, pustular plaques in the beard area. Hairs in the affected area are easily removed. What does the ease of hair removal indicate, and what therapy is required?","choices":["Tinea barbae; oral antifungal therapy is required","Tinea barbae; a topical cream is enough","Pseudofolliculitis barbae; change shaving","Acne vulgaris; a topical retinoid","Bacterial folliculitis; oral cephalexin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hair is easily removed in tinea barbae unlike in bacterial folliculitis, and a bacterial culture can be done to rule the latter out. Topical antifungals do not penetrate the hair follicle, so oral therapy with griseofulvin or terbinafine is required. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 21-year-old woman presents with a rash on her trunk. Ten days before the current eruption she noticed a single oval, sharply demarcated salmon-coloured plaque about 4 cm across on her upper back that became scaly and began to clear in the centre. Now numerous smaller similar lesions have appeared over the trunk, with their long axes aligned along the lines of the ribs. She feels well. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Nummular eczema","Secondary syphilis","Tinea corporis","Guttate psoriasis","Pityriasis rosea"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. A herald patch occurs in 50 to 90 percent of cases, preceding the exanthem: a single 2 to 5 cm round or oval, sharply demarcated pink or salmon-coloured plaque on the chest, neck, or back that clears centrally, leaving a collarette of scale. The eruption of smaller similar lesions follows 1 to 2 weeks later. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 169"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has confirmed dermatophyte onychomycosis of two fingernails and four toenails. What is the usual duration of first-line oral terbinafine for each, and what safety review is required?","choices":["Six weeks for both; check renal function","Two weeks for both; no monitoring","Six weeks fingernails, twelve toenails; check liver","Twelve weeks fingernails, six toenails; check liver","Lifelong therapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Oral terbinafine is first-line for most dermatophyte nail disease, usually 6 weeks for fingernails and 12 weeks for toenails, with hepatic disease and interactions reviewed and baseline liver tests obtained per labelling and patient risk. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 26-year-old Black man reports tender bumps on his neck and jawline that appear after shaving. Examination shows erythematous papules, several with a visible central hair shaft curving back into the skin. What is the underlying process?","choices":["Follicular hyperkeratinisation with excess sebum","Apocrine gland occlusion","An allergic reaction to shaving products","Follicular infection by Staphylococcus aureus","A foreign body reaction to re-entrant hair"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Pseudofolliculitis barbae is a foreign body reaction to hair in any shaved area. The cut hair curves into the follicular wall and penetrates the skin to form a tender red papule or pustule, and it occurs commonly in Black men because of tightly curled facial hair together with keratin gene variations. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man with suspected Rocky Mountain spotted fever has blood drawn before treatment is started. What laboratory abnormalities are characteristic, and what is the gold standard diagnostic test?","choices":["Thrombocytopenia, hyponatraemia, normal white count","Leukocytosis with thrombocytosis","Hypernatraemia and hyperglycaemia","Eosinophilia with raised immunoglobulin E","A positive potassium hydroxide preparation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Those are the characteristic laboratory findings, and the indirect immunofluorescence assay is the gold standard. Cerebrospinal fluid may show leukocytosis, moderately elevated protein, and normal glucose. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman has several beige to dark brown papules and plaques 5 to 15 mm on the trunk that feel velvety and appear stuck onto the skin surface. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Seborrheic keratoses","Dermatosis papulosa nigrans","Malignant melanoma","Congenital melanocytic naevi","Solar lentigines"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Seborrheic keratoses are benign papules and plaques, beige to brown to black, 2 to 20 mm in diameter, feeling velvety or warty and appearing stuck or pasted onto the skin. They are common in older adults and are easily mistaken for neoplasms. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman has a sharply bounded eruption on the forearm whose outline does not correspond to any anatomical distribution. What general feature most often allows contact dermatitis to be distinguished from other dermatologic conditions on inspection alone?","choices":["Its symmetric involvement of flexural surfaces","Its clearly demarcated, unnatural patterns","Its association with a preceding viral prodrome","Its predilection for sebum-rich areas of the body","Its tendency to produce lesions with central clearing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Contact dermatitis in general is often distinguishable from other dermatologic conditions because of its clearly demarcated, unnatural patterns — the eruption traces the shape of whatever touched the skin rather than following a biological distribution. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 79"}]};
